From: Adriel Santos <adriel.turox@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] remoteproc: qcom_q6v5_mpd: tear down user PD when root PD is dead

A fatal error in the root PD leaves the Q6 firmware gone while the user
PDs still read RPROC_RUNNING. wcss_pd_stop() then still issues the SMP2P
stop request, whose ack can only come from the firmware that just died,
so it times out and the error return aborts the teardown before the MSA
unlock and before rproc_shutdown() drops the root PD's power refcount.

The root PD is consequently pinned forever: its recovery rproc_start()
fails with -ETIMEDOUT, both radios stay dead, and only a reboot brings
them back. Reproduced on IPQ5018 (2.4 GHz internal + QCN6122 5 GHz) with
"echo assert > .../simulate_fw_crash": the stop write returns -ETIMEDOUT
after 5 s and the user PD state stays "running".

Skip the handshake when the root PD is crashed or offline and tear the
user PD down locally; the user PD's firmware is dead in that case
anyway. Behaviour is unchanged whenever the root PD is alive, which is
every normal stop: with several user PDs booted the root PD only leaves
RPROC_RUNNING once the last of them has already been torn down.

Signed-off-by: Adriel Santos <adriel.turox@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5_mpd.c
+++ b/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5_mpd.c
@@ -242,9 +242,22 @@
 	struct rproc *rpd_rproc = dev_get_drvdata(upd->dev->parent);
 	struct q6_wcss *wcss = rpd_rproc->priv;
 	u32 pasid = (upd->pd_asid << 8) | UPD_SWID;
+	bool rootpd_dead = rpd_rproc->state == RPROC_CRASHED ||
+			   rpd_rproc->state == RPROC_OFFLINE;
 	int ret;
 
-	if (rproc->state != RPROC_CRASHED && upd->q6.stop_bit) {
+	/*
+	 * Stopping a user PD is an SMP2P handshake answered by the Q6 firmware.
+	 * If the *root* PD has taken a fatal error the firmware is already gone,
+	 * so the stop-ack can never arrive: qcom_q6v5_request_stop() only burns
+	 * its timeout and the error return below aborts the teardown before the
+	 * MSA unlock and the rproc_shutdown() that drops the root PD's power
+	 * refcount. The root PD can then never be restarted -- rproc_start()
+	 * fails with -ETIMEDOUT for as long as the box is up -- and the only way
+	 * to get the radios back is a reboot. The user PD's firmware is dead
+	 * either way, so skip the handshake and tear it down locally.
+	 */
+	if (rproc->state != RPROC_CRASHED && !rootpd_dead && upd->q6.stop_bit) {
 		ret = qcom_q6v5_request_stop(&upd->q6, NULL);
 		if (ret) {
 			dev_err(upd->dev, "pd not stopped\n");
